1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,277
This is STAT 331, video 8. The topic is hypothesis tests and p-values for the slope beta 1.

2
00:00:08,727 --> 00:00:30,679
After this video, you can test a null hypothesis about beta 1 with the t statistic. You can calculate and interpret a p-value, two-sided or one-sided. You can read the coefficient table. And you can solve Tutorial 1 question 2, parts c and d, and Assignment 1, question 2 a and question 4.

3
00:00:31,129 --> 00:00:48,388
We want to test a null hypothesis, H 0: beta 1 equals theta 0. The alternative is H 1: beta 1 is not equal to theta 0. Theta 0 is a fixed number that we choose. Often theta 0 is 0.

4
00:00:48,838 --> 00:01:06,182
Suppose H 0 is true. Then beta 1 hat has a normal distribution with mean theta 0 and variance sigma squared over S x x. This is the purple curve. If our estimate falls near theta 0, the data are not surprising.

5
00:01:06,632 --> 00:01:20,755
If our estimate falls far out in a tail, the data would be very surprising if H 0 were true. So the goal of a test is to measure this surprise: how extreme are our data, if H 0 is true?

6
00:01:21,205 --> 00:01:31,487
To measure the distance from theta 0, we use a test statistic. On the beta 1 scale, we mark theta 0 and our estimate beta 1 hat.

7
00:01:31,937 --> 00:01:45,207
The raw distance is beta 1 hat minus theta 0. But this number has units. It also depends on the noise in the data. So the raw distance alone does not tell us if it is large.

8
00:01:45,657 --> 00:01:58,990
We measure the distance in standard errors. The standard error of beta 1 hat is sigma hat over the square root of S x x. In this picture, the distance is two and a half standard errors.

9
00:01:59,440 --> 00:02:13,563
So we divide by the standard error. The result is the t statistic. Here t equals 2.5. Under H 0, this statistic has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

10
00:02:14,013 --> 00:02:24,957
This comes from the last two videos. Beta 1 hat minus beta 1, over sigma over root S x x, is standard normal. But sigma is unknown.

11
00:02:25,407 --> 00:02:41,044
We put sigma hat in place of sigma. Sigma hat squared is the sum of squared residuals over n minus 2. Then the ratio has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. It has wider tails than the normal.

12
00:02:41,494 --> 00:02:54,337
Under H 0, beta 1 equals theta 0. So we can put theta 0 in place of beta 1. Then T contains only the data and theta 0. We can calculate it.

13
00:02:54,787 --> 00:03:11,981
The p-value answers this question: if H 0 is true, what is the probability of a test statistic as extreme as, or more extreme than, the one we observed? Here is an example with 10 degrees of freedom, and t observed equals 2.1.

14
00:03:12,431 --> 00:03:23,269
More extreme means farther from 0. First, the right tail: the probability that T is at least 2.1. This area is 0.031.

15
00:03:23,719 --> 00:03:31,164
A value below minus 2.1 is just as extreme. By symmetry, the left tail has the same area.

16
00:03:31,614 --> 00:03:49,363
So the p-value is two times the right tail. 2 times 0.031 is 0.062. In formula form, p equals 2 times 1 minus P of T less than or equal to the absolute value of t observed.

17
00:03:49,813 --> 00:04:04,747
Slide 42 writes minus absolute t inside the last probability. That is a sign slip. Use plus absolute t, or equivalently, 2 times P of T less than or equal to minus absolute t.

18
00:04:05,197 --> 00:04:23,863
Now the decision. We choose a level alpha, usually 0.05. The two red tails together have area 0.05. They start at the quantile t 0.975 with 10 degrees of freedom, which is 2.228.

19
00:04:24,313 --> 00:04:37,199
Our t of 2.1 is inside the critical values. Its p-value is 0.062, above 0.05. So we do not reject H 0.

20
00:04:37,649 --> 00:04:57,979
If t were 3.0, it would fall in a tail. Its p-value is 0.013, below 0.05. Then we reject H 0. The two rules always agree: p is below alpha exactly when the absolute value of t is above the critical value.

21
00:04:58,429 --> 00:05:16,136
Here is the example from the slides. The data are 236 people, with brain weight y in grams and head size x in cubic centimeters. We test H 0: beta 1 equals 0, against beta 1 not equal to 0.

22
00:05:16,586 --> 00:05:40,437
Beta 1 hat is 0.2608. Its standard error is 0.01307. So t observed is 0.2608 over 0.01307, which is 19.957. The critical value with 234 degrees of freedom is 1.97.

23
00:05:40,887 --> 00:05:55,991
The t value is far beyond the curve. The p-value is 2 times P of T greater than 19.957. It is 2.03 times 10 to the power minus 52. This is almost zero.

24
00:05:56,441 --> 00:06:11,502
The p-value is below 0.05. So we reject the null hypothesis at the 5 percent level. The data give very strong evidence of a linear association between head size and brain weight.

25
00:06:11,952 --> 00:06:19,909
The slide also gives the coefficient table for this fit. The course does not need the code. But you must read the numbers.

26
00:06:20,359 --> 00:06:35,399
Each row is one coefficient. The Estimate column gives beta 1 hat, 0.26082. The Std. Error column gives its standard error, 0.01307.

27
00:06:35,849 --> 00:06:56,159
The t value column is the estimate over the standard error: 0.26082 over 0.01307 is 19.957. So the t value counts the standard errors from 0 to beta 1 hat. Here there are about 20 of them.

28
00:06:56,609 --> 00:07:17,195
The last column is the two-sided p-value for H 0: beta 1 equals 0. Less than 2 times 10 to the minus 16 means it is too small to print. This table always tests theta 0 equal to 0. For other values of theta 0, calculate t yourself.

29
00:07:17,645 --> 00:07:31,789
The intercept row tests H 0: beta 0 equals 0. Its t value is 335.43 over 47.78, which is 7.021.

30
00:07:32,239 --> 00:07:45,295
The residual standard error is sigma hat, 72.35. The degrees of freedom are n minus 2, which is 234. So n is 236.

31
00:07:45,745 --> 00:07:58,759
What if the p-value is above 0.05? Would we accept the null? No. We only say: we do not have enough evidence to reject it. Here is an example with our own numbers.

32
00:07:59,209 --> 00:08:13,097
The 95 percent confidence interval is 0.4 plus or minus 2.086 times 0.3. That is from minus 0.23 to 1.03.

33
00:08:13,547 --> 00:08:20,395
The value 0 is inside the interval. So the test does not reject beta 1 equals 0.

34
00:08:20,845 --> 00:08:36,653
But 0.5 and 0.9 are also inside the interval. The data agree with all of them. So we cannot conclude that beta 1 is 0. We can only say that the data do not show that it is different from 0.

35
00:08:37,103 --> 00:08:53,316
Does the p-value equal the probability that beta 1 equals 0? No. The p-value starts from an assumption. We assume H 0 is true. Then T has a known t curve, and the p-value is the tail area beyond our t.

36
00:08:53,766 --> 00:09:03,238
Beta 1 is a fixed, unknown constant. It is not random. So the p-value is not the probability that H 0 is true.

37
00:09:03,688 --> 00:09:21,565
The correct meaning: under the null hypothesis, the p-value is the probability of a test statistic as extreme as the one we observed. A small p-value says our data would be rare under H 0. That is why it is evidence against H 0.

38
00:09:22,015 --> 00:09:36,394
Now suppose we use this rule: reject H 0 whenever the p-value is below 5 percent. Suppose H 0 is true, and we repeat the experiment many times. Each time, we calculate t.

39
00:09:36,844 --> 00:09:46,828
Each dot is one simulated experiment with 10 points, where the true beta 1 is 0. Most t values are between the critical values.

40
00:09:47,278 --> 00:09:56,771
Here are 40 experiments. 2 of them fall in the red tails. In those experiments, we reject H 0, but H 0 is true.

41
00:09:57,221 --> 00:10:12,069
This mistake is a type one error. With the 5 percent rule, the long-run proportion of type one errors is 5 percent. This is the type one error rate. Here, 2 out of 40 is exactly 5 percent.

42
00:10:12,519 --> 00:10:22,951
So far the alternative was two-sided: beta 1 is not equal to theta 0. Evidence against H 0 can then point in either direction.

43
00:10:23,401 --> 00:10:32,639
With two sides, both tails count. For t equal to 2.1 with 10 degrees of freedom, p is 0.062.

44
00:10:33,089 --> 00:10:50,902
Sometimes we care about one direction only. For example, H 1: beta 1 is greater than theta 0. Then only large values of beta 1 hat minus theta 0 are evidence for H 1. So only large values of T count.

45
00:10:51,352 --> 00:11:08,717
So the p-value includes only one tail: P of T at least t observed. Here it is 0.031, half of the two-sided value. If t observed is negative, the one-sided p-value is above one half, and we do not reject.

46
00:11:09,167 --> 00:11:17,381
Here is the procedure as five steps. Step 1: write the hypotheses with the value theta 0 from the question.

47
00:11:17,831 --> 00:11:24,700
Step 2: calculate t observed. Beta 1 hat minus theta 0, over the standard error.

48
00:11:25,150 --> 00:11:30,291
Step 3: the reference distribution is t with n minus 2 degrees of freedom.

49
00:11:30,741 --> 00:11:43,776
Step 4: find the p-value. Use two tails for a two-sided alternative, and one tail for a one-sided alternative. Or compare the absolute value of t with the critical value.

50
00:11:44,226 --> 00:11:53,954
Step 5: conclude in words, in the context of the question. Say reject or do not reject, and say what that means for the variables.

51
00:11:54,404 --> 00:12:04,879
Now the tutorial. Question 2 has 100 aircraft. y is the maintenance cost in thousands of dollars. x is the number of flight hours.

52
00:12:05,329 --> 00:12:21,713
The question gives this output. The row for hours gives beta 1 hat equals 6.410 and a standard error of 0.499. There are 98 degrees of freedom, because n minus 2 is 98.

53
00:12:22,163 --> 00:12:29,992
On the beta 1 scale, 0 is about 12.85 standard errors away from the estimate. That is far.

54
00:12:30,442 --> 00:12:40,789
Part d asks about the value 6. It is very close to the estimate, less than one standard error away. Pause the video and try parts c and d.

55
00:12:41,239 --> 00:12:50,881
Part c. Significance of beta 1 means: test H 0, beta 1 equals 0, against beta 1 not equal to 0.

56
00:12:51,331 --> 00:13:03,107
The t statistic is 6.410 minus 0, over 0.499. That is 12.85. The output prints it as 12.8.

57
00:13:03,557 --> 00:13:16,507
Under H 0, T has a t distribution with 98 degrees of freedom. The critical value t 0.975, 98 is 1.984.

58
00:13:16,957 --> 00:13:27,645
12.85 is much larger than 1.984. The p-value is less than 2 times 10 to the minus 16, from the last column.

59
00:13:28,095 --> 00:13:40,596
So we reject H 0 at the 5 percent level. There is strong evidence that the mean maintenance cost changes with the number of flight hours. The estimate says the cost increases.

60
00:13:41,046 --> 00:13:59,947
Part d. y is in thousands of dollars. So an increase of 6000 dollars per hour is beta 1 equals 6. This is the most common mistake: theta 0 is 6, not 6000. The test is H 0: beta 1 equals 6, two-sided.

61
00:14:00,397 --> 00:14:16,760
The t statistic is 6.410 minus 6, over 0.499. That is 0.82. The table cannot give this value: its t value always uses theta 0 equal to 0.

62
00:14:17,210 --> 00:14:23,930
0.82 is well inside the critical values plus and minus 1.984.

63
00:14:24,380 --> 00:14:33,319
The p-value is 2 times P of T at least 0.82, with 98 degrees of freedom. It is 0.41.

64
00:14:33,769 --> 00:14:51,966
The p-value is above 0.05. So we do not reject H 0 at the 5 percent level. The data agree with an average increase of 6000 dollars per flight hour. This does not prove that the increase is exactly 6000 dollars.

65
00:14:52,416 --> 00:15:08,053
Assignment 1, question 2 a. We reject H 0, beta 1 equals 0, at the 0.05 level. What does that say about the 95 percent confidence interval? Pause the video and try it.

66
00:15:08,503 --> 00:15:26,210
The top line is the t scale. We do not reject when the absolute value of t is at most t star. t star is t 0.975 with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. Here is a t of 1.4, inside the green region.

67
00:15:26,660 --> 00:15:46,329
The bottom line is the beta 1 scale. The confidence interval is beta 1 hat plus or minus t star times the standard error. Its half-width is t star standard errors. Beta 1 hat is only 1.4 standard errors from 0. So the interval contains 0.

68
00:15:46,779 --> 00:15:53,862
Now take t equal to 3.2. It is outside the green region, so we reject H 0.

69
00:15:54,312 --> 00:16:05,512
Beta 1 hat is now 3.2 standard errors from 0. The half-width is only t star standard errors. So the interval does not contain 0.

70
00:16:05,962 --> 00:16:26,079
In symbols: the absolute value of t is above t star, exactly when the absolute value of beta 1 hat is above t star times the standard error, exactly when 0 is outside the interval. So the answer: the 95 percent confidence interval does not contain 0.

71
00:16:26,529 --> 00:16:45,580
We can check this result with Tutorial 1. The 95 percent confidence interval for beta 1 is 6.410 plus or minus 1.984 times 0.499. That is from 5.42 to 7.40.

72
00:16:46,030 --> 00:16:53,539
The value 0 is outside the interval. This matches part c, where we rejected beta 1 equals 0.

73
00:16:53,989 --> 00:17:14,171
The value 6 is inside the interval. This matches part d, where we did not reject beta 1 equals 6. So a two-sided test at level alpha rejects theta 0 exactly when theta 0 is outside the 100 times 1 minus alpha percent confidence interval.

74
00:17:14,621 --> 00:17:26,930
Assignment 1, question 4, is a simulation. The true model has 10 fixed x values, and normal errors. Step one: simulate 10 values of y from this model.

75
00:17:27,380 --> 00:17:40,863
Here is one simulated data set, with beta 1 equal to 0.25 and sigma equal to 1. The gray dashed line is the true line, 1 plus 0.25 x.

76
00:17:41,313 --> 00:17:51,745
Step two: fit the least squares line to the simulated data. The green line is the fit. It is not equal to the true line, because of the random errors.

77
00:17:52,195 --> 00:18:02,371
Step three: calculate the t statistic and the two-sided p-value for H 0: beta 1 equals 0. There are 8 degrees of freedom.

78
00:18:02,821 --> 00:18:17,199
Step four: repeat this 2000 times, and keep each p-value. The assignment does this in R. This video shows only the results. We used our own random seed, so your numbers will be a little different.

79
00:18:17,649 --> 00:18:31,025
Part b. Beta 1 equals 0.25 and sigma equals 1. The histogram shows the 2000 p-values. The red bar is the p-values below 0.05.

80
00:18:31,475 --> 00:18:43,955
Many p-values are small, but not all. We reject H 0 in 48.8 percent of the samples. So in about half of the samples, the test misses a real slope.

81
00:18:44,405 --> 00:18:48,992
Part c. Now beta 1 equals 0, so H 0 is true.

82
00:18:49,442 --> 00:19:02,711
The p-values are spread evenly from 0 to 1. The histogram is flat. We reject in 4.9 percent of the samples. This is close to 5 percent: the type one error rate.

83
00:19:03,161 --> 00:19:10,180
Part d repeats b and c with a smaller error standard deviation, sigma equals 0.5.

84
00:19:10,630 --> 00:19:22,577
With beta 1 equal to 0.25, almost all p-values are below 0.05. We reject in 97.2 percent of the samples.

85
00:19:23,027 --> 00:19:27,720
With beta 1 equal to 0, the null hypothesis is true again.

86
00:19:28,170 --> 00:19:42,826
The histogram is flat again. We reject in 4.1 percent of the samples. This is about 5 percent. With 2000 samples, values from about 4 to 6 percent are normal random variation.

87
00:19:43,276 --> 00:20:08,087
Part e asks us to explain. Look at the t values. The standard deviation of beta 1 hat is sigma over root S x x. Here S x x is 76. With sigma equal to 1, the true slope is 2.18 standard deviations from 0. The t values center near this value. About half of them pass the critical value 2.306.

88
00:20:08,537 --> 00:20:23,577
With sigma equal to 0.5, the standard deviation is half as large. The true slope is now 4.36 standard deviations from 0. So almost all t values pass the critical value.

89
00:20:24,027 --> 00:20:43,568
So the explanation. When H 0 is true, T has a t distribution with 8 degrees of freedom, for any sigma. So we reject about 5 percent of the time. When beta 1 is not 0, a smaller sigma gives a more precise estimate, a larger t, and more rejections.

90
00:20:44,018 --> 00:21:01,042
To recap. The t statistic is beta 1 hat minus theta 0, over the standard error. Compare it with t with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. A two-sided p-value uses two tails, and a one-sided p-value uses one tail.

91
00:21:01,492 --> 00:21:11,583
Reject when p is below alpha. Not rejecting does not prove H 0. And the p-value is not the probability that beta 1 is 0.

92
00:21:12,033 --> 00:21:26,838
The coefficient table tests theta 0 equal to 0 only. Watch the units of theta 0. A two-sided test rejects theta 0 exactly when theta 0 is outside the confidence interval.

93
00:21:27,288 --> 00:21:38,424
When H 0 is true, the p-values are spread evenly, so the type one error rate is alpha. The next video estimates the mean response at a given x.
